To find the decimal expansion of $8/11$, we perform long division:
\[
\begin{array}{c|cccccccc}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0.}&7&2&7&2&7&2&7 \\
\cline{2-9}
11 & 8. & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{7.} & 2 & 7 & 2 & 7 & 2 & 7 \\
\cline{2-8}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{0} & 7 & 2 & 7 & 2 & 7 & 2 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 7 & 7 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
\cline{3-8}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 0 & 2 & 7 & 2 & 7 & 2 \\
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\
\cline{4-8}
\multicolumn{2}{r}{} & 0 & 2 & 7 & 2 & 7 & 2 \\
\end{array}
\]
We see that the decimal expansion of $8/11$ is $0.\overline{72}$, where the repeating block is $72$.
So the least number of digits in a repeating block is $\boxed{2}$.The answer is: 2

The arithmetic mean of two numbers is the sum of the numbers divided by 2.
So, the arithmetic mean of $\frac{2}{5}$ and $\frac{4}{7}$ is $\frac{\frac{2}{5} + \frac{4}{7}}{2} = \frac{\frac{14}{35} + \frac{20}{35}}{2} = \frac{\frac{34}{35}}{2} = \boxed{\frac{17}{35}}$.
The answer is: \frac{17}{35}

